course: religion-ethics
lesson: 44
title: Cheating, rules and games
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cheating means breaking the rules in secret to get ahead, such as copying answers in a test, bending the rules of a game, or an athlete taking banned drugs.
explain: Picture a spelling test where one child glances at a neighbour's paper: even if nobody notices, the mark is a lie about what that child knows, and it is unfair to everyone who learnt the words honestly.
explain: Not all help is cheating: a friend explaining a sum helps you learn, but a parent writing your homework for you does not; the line is whether the work and the learning are really yours.
explain: Rules matter, but some rules are unfair and should be challenged respectfully, and sometimes a more important duty, like fetching help for a hurt child, outweighs a small rule.
explain: Christians believe God sees what is done in secret and values honesty and humility, so a good player wins without boasting, loses without sulking, and would rather lose fairly than win by cheating.
fact: Cheating is breaking the rules in secret to gain an unfair advantage.
fact: Cheating in a test is unfair to others even if nobody finds out, and it hides what you really know.
fact: If you see a friend cheat, loyalty and fairness clash; a good first step is often to talk to your friend.
fact: A game is only fun and only a real win when everyone plays by the same rules.
fact: Doping means athletes taking banned drugs to perform better; it cheats honest competitors and can badly harm health.
fact: Help that lets you learn, such as an explanation, is fine; someone doing your work for you is cheating.
fact: Rules about using AI for homework differ between schools and are still debated; being honest about what you did is always required.
fact: Some rules are unfair, for example a rule that favours one group; the fair response is to challenge them respectfully, not to break them secretly.
fact: Sometimes a more important duty outweighs a rule, such as running in the corridor to fetch help for a hurt child.
fact: Sportsmanship means playing fairly, winning without boasting and losing without sulking.
fact: A good loser shakes hands, congratulates the winner and learns from the game.
fact: When his disciples were criticised for picking grain on the Sabbath, Jesus said "the Sabbath was made for people, not people for the Sabbath" (Mark 2:27), and he went on to heal a man on the Sabbath (Mark 3:1-5): rules are there to serve people.
fact: Christians believe God sees what is done in secret (Matthew 6:4), so honesty matters even when no one else is watching.
exercise: What is cheating? | Breaking the rules in secret to gain an unfair advantage | Losing a game because the other side played better | Asking a teacher to explain something you do not understand | Practising hard every day to get better at a sport
exercise: Why is cheating in a test unfair even if you never get caught? | It is unfair to honest pupils and hides what you really know | It is only unfair if the teacher finds out about it later | It is not unfair, because tests do not really matter in the end anyway | It is unfair only if you copy from your best friend
exercise: You see your best friend cheating in a test. What is the best-reasoned first step? | Talk to your friend first, because fairness matters but you also care about them | Shout out in the middle of the test so everyone knows | Do nothing, because friends must always protect each other | Copy your friend too, because then it is fair for both of you
exercise: Why does winning a game by cheating not feel like real winning? | Because you did not win by the rules everyone agreed to | Because winning never feels good, whatever happens and however you play | Because the other players were not really trying very hard that day | Because the prize is always smaller for people who are caught cheating
exercise: Why do sports have rules against doping? | Doping cheats honest athletes and can badly harm health | Doping makes sport far too slow and boring for people to watch | Doping is only banned because the drugs cost such a lot of money | Doping is allowed; sports have no rules about it, only about kit
exercise: Is it cheating if a friend explains a sum you are stuck on? | No, help that lets you learn is fine | Yes, any help at all is always cheating | Yes, unless the friend is older than you | No, but only if the friend gets paid for it
exercise: Is it cheating if a parent writes your homework for you? | Yes, because the work is not yours and you do not learn | No, because parents are allowed to do anything they like for you | No, as long as your parent is really good at that school subject | Yes, but only if the teacher gives it a high mark or a gold star
exercise: When might using AI for homework be fair? | When the school's rules allow it and you are honest about how you used it | Always, because a computer is just a tool like a pencil, so anything goes | Never, under any rules, whatever the teacher says | Only when you do not tell anyone you used it
exercise: Why are rules about AI and homework described as debated? | Schools have different rules and people still argue about what is fair | Because AI is never, ever allowed in any school anywhere in the whole world | Because nobody has ever thought about the question before | Because only teachers are allowed to use computers
exercise: Your school has a rule that only the older pupils may use the new field. What is the best way to respond? | Ask the head teacher respectfully to change it and give reasons | Sneak onto the field at break whenever the teachers are not watching | Complain about the teachers to everyone in a nasty way | Accept it silently, because every rule must be fair
exercise: Why is it better to challenge an unfair rule than secretly break it? | Challenging it can change it for everyone and keeps trust | Secretly breaking it is braver and works better | Breaking it is fine as long as you are not caught | Rules never change, so there is no point challenging them
exercise: A child falls and hits their head, and you run in the corridor to fetch a teacher. Was this right? | Yes, helping a hurt child is a more important duty than the running rule | No, a rule is a rule, so you should have walked slowly | Yes, because running in the corridor is always allowed | No, you should have waited until someone else noticed
exercise: What did Jesus teach about the Sabbath rules in Mark 2 and 3? | The Sabbath was made for people, so rules are there to serve people | Rules do not matter at all, so anyone may break any rule whenever they like | The Sabbath rule was only for people who were not hungry | Healing on the Sabbath was wrong, so he stopped doing it
exercise: What is sportsmanship? | Playing fairly, winning without boasting and losing without sulking | Winning at any cost, even if it means bending the rules when nobody looks | Being the best player on the team every single time | Never playing sport so that nobody ever has to lose
exercise: What does a good loser do? | Shakes hands, congratulates the winner and learns from the game | Blames the referee loudly and says the other team must have cheated | Walks off the pitch without speaking to anyone at all, not even the team | Says the game was stupid anyway and refuses ever to play it again
exercise: Why do Christians think honesty matters even when no one is watching? | They believe God sees what is done in secret | They believe teachers always see what is done | They believe cheating is only wrong in church | They believe honesty is only needed with adults
test: Your mate whispers in the spelling test, "Psst, what's number 6? Nobody's looking." What is the best-reasoned response? | Don't tell him; it's unfair on the others, and offer to practise with him after | Tell him, because a friend in need comes before any rule | Tell the teacher at once, loudly, so that he gets into really big trouble in front of everyone | Tell him the wrong answer on purpose to teach him a lesson
test: Your brother brags, "I moved my counter two squares when you weren't looking, so I won Monopoly, ha!" What is the best reply? | That's not a real win, the game only works if we both play by the rules | Well done, being sneaky is part of every game | Fine, I'll cheat even more next time to get you back, and then we're even | Games don't have real rules, so it doesn't matter
test: Your cousin says, "That runner took drugs but he still ran the race himself, so it's not cheating." Is he right? | No, banned drugs give an unfair edge over honest runners and can harm health | Yes, as long as he did all of the running himself with his own legs, it is fair | Yes, because all runners take drugs anyway | No, but only because the drugs are expensive
test: Your friend says, "My dad did my whole project, but I coloured it in, so it's mine." What is the best answer? | If your dad did the work, it isn't really yours and you didn't learn it | Yes, colouring it in makes the whole project yours | Yes, because dads are allowed to do homework for their children whenever they like | It's fine as long as the teacher gives it a gold star
test: A classmate says, "I'll just get the AI to write my story, Miss will never know." What is the best-reasoned reply? | Check what the school allows; passing off AI writing as your own isn't honest | Go for it, it's not cheating if a computer does it | You're a cheat and I'm never speaking to you again | Only do it if you change a few of the words so it looks more like your own writing
test: A boy says, "The rule says girls can't play football at lunch. Rubbish rule, let's just ignore it." What is the best response? | It sounds unfair; let's ask the teachers together to change it | Ignore it and play anyway, because unfair rules simply don't count | Accept it, because rules made by teachers are always fair to everyone | Tell the girls to stop moaning about it, because it's the rule
test: Your little sister says, "Jesus broke the Sabbath rules, so we can break any rule we want." How do you put her right? | He healed a sick man, showing rules should serve people, not that any rule can be broken | She is right, Jesus taught that no rules matter at all | Jesus never did anything on the Sabbath, so she made it up | Jesus said only grown-ups need to keep rules
test: After a match, a player on the other team sulks: "Ref's blind, you lot only won cos of luck, whatever." Which shows good sportsmanship in reply? | Say "good game" and offer to shake hands anyway | Laugh at him and tell everyone he's a sore loser | Say "you're rubbish and you always will be" | Tell him to go home and never come back
test: A classmate who is not religious asks, "If nobody can see you cheat, why bother being honest?" What is the most thoughtful answer? | Because cheating is still unfair to others and fools yourself; I also believe God sees | Because you will definitely get caught one day | Because teachers secretly have cameras everywhere | You shouldn't bother, it only matters if you get caught
test: You see a Year 6 boy slip on wet stairs and he can't get up. The rule is "no running". What is the best choice? | Run to get a teacher, because his safety matters more than the rule | Walk slowly, because breaking a rule is always wrong, whatever happens | Do nothing, because it is the dinner ladies' job | Tell him to get up and stop making a fuss
test: A judge at the olympiad asks: "Is it ever right to break a rule?" What is the best-reasoned answer? | Sometimes, when a more important duty like saving someone outweighs it, but not just because it suits me | Never, because rules are rules and that is the end of it | Always, whenever the rule feels annoying or boring | Only when you are sure that nobody will find out
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the practice termed "doping" in competitive sport? | The ingestion by athletes of prohibited substances with a view to enhancing performance | The provision of water to athletes during the course of a competition | The training of athletes under the supervision of a qualified coach | The selection of athletes for a team by means of a ballot
exam: Identify the principal criterion by which legitimate assistance with homework is to be distinguished from cheating. | Whether the assistance enables the pupil to learn, the work remaining the pupil's own | Whether the assistance is rendered by a person older than the pupil | Whether the assistance is rendered free of charge | Whether the assistance results in a mark of the highest order
exam: Given that a school rule is considered unfair to a particular group of pupils, select the response most consistent with sound ethical reasoning. | The rule ought to be challenged by respectful representation to the competent authority | The rule ought to be disregarded covertly by all pupils affected | The rule ought to be accepted without question in all circumstances | The rule ought to be made the subject of personal abuse directed at the staff
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the saying "the Sabbath was made for people, not people for the Sabbath" (Mark 2:27). | It signifies that religious rules exist to serve the welfare of human beings | It signifies that the observance of the Sabbath was entirely abolished by Jesus | It signifies that human beings exist solely to observe the Sabbath | It signifies that the Sabbath is to be observed only by the clergy
